{"description":"Publishing a content revision of an existing line re-initialises its state vector from the authoring template's initial values. Observed on eka/ctr:hardening-roadmap: revision 1 was container-state active; a content revision (corrected workItems) published as instance 2 came back container-state planned, with the depends-on plan still locked and the corpus text unchanged.","impact":"Any content correction on a live container silently reverts its lifecycle: work-item transitions start refusing with 'not registered in the current active container' because membership is computed over ACTIVE containers, and a completed container can be un-completed by fixing a typo in its objective. Nothing reports it — publish succeeds and prints only the new instance number, so the regression is discovered later as an unexplained transition refusal.","acceptanceCriteria":"- A content revision of an existing line preserves the current state vector and appends only the content change-log entry; initial values apply to instance 1.\n- Or, if the reset is intended, publish refuses a content revision whose state would regress (container not planned) and names what the caller must do, instead of succeeding quietly.\n- A test covers the round trip: activate a container, publish a content revision, assert container-state is still active and the plan stays locked."}